7592186370651} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430044}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Surface Area of Revolution"} :step/id #uuid "86ce5fe6-7a22-5926-9ab2-a82592ddf97f" :step/math-academy-id 33278} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130113 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129217 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129036 :question/id #uuid "fcfc9339-efd3-525f-a6f3-695124724e1c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9120"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e70c003-febf-5ec6-bc57-224282eb5bbc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050393}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129414} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244249} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262631} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430041}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of Y"} :step/id #uuid "c6514f45-14b6-5849-8cf5-f67e8581faff" :step/math-academy-id 33991 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130112}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130114 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130413 :tutorial/content "Consider the arc of the curve $y=f(x)$ that lies between the vertical lines $x=x_1$ and $x=x_2,$ as shown below on the left. By rotating this curve $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis, we get a **surface of revolution,** as shown below on the right. How can we find its surface area?\n\n![](images/d9/d929311a1d86da82fd2acb517ed148715273d37a66928f0cb2949353e06457e1.png)\n\nFirst, we divide the interval $[a,b]$ into small subintervals, each of length $\\Delta{x}.$ Let's consider a part of the curve that corresponds to one of those subintervals and let the length of the corresponding curved arc be $\\Delta{s}.$ When this part of the curve is rotated about the $x$-axis, it forms a closed strip. \n\n![](images/55/55ff01b354818b7ec3b9240d470acfa14c648b967f142b7554c172d12e2ca57b.png)\n\nIf $\\Delta{x}$ is very small, we can assume that both left and right radii of the strip are approximately equal to $y=f(x).$ Then, this strip can be viewed as the lateral surface of a right cylinder with height $h=\\Delta{s}$ and radius $r=y=f(x).$ \n\n![](images/5a/5a71ce7729d37ca3d1fb86067f0d3f2233243a24710d08132dfc63f372bb6521.png)\n\nWe can approximate the lateral surface area of such a cylinder as\n\n$$\nS_{\\text{strip}} \\approx S_{\\text{cylinder}} = 2\\pi r \\cdot h = 2\\pi y \\cdot \\Delta{s}.\n$$\n\nThe sum over all subintervals will approximate the total area $S$ that we want. The approximation becomes more and more accurate as $\\Delta{s} \\to 0$. Therefore, to get the area of the surface, we need to integrate:\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s\n$$\n\nRecall that for a curve $y = f(x),$ the arc length element $\\textrm d s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can find the total surface area using the formula\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} y \\sqrt{1+ \\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f7ed0d58-7855-54cd-952c-8f23fe9c6678" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5532 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9fe394ca-d35a-573c-8c77-ab458259c696" :step/math-academy-id 34375 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130111}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130115 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130414 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we're given a curve of the form $x=f(y)$ that lies between the horizontal lines $y=y_1$ and $y=y_2.$ How do we calculate the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis?\n\nEarlier, we showed that the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nFor a curve $x = f(y),$ it can be shown that the arc length element $\\textrm d s$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d y}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the surface that is obtained when the $x=f(y)$ curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis is given by the formula\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{y_1}^{y_2} y \\sqrt{1+ \\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}y.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1edfe684-3e13-5317-b7d2-d7f6cf246298" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5534 :tutorial/title "Surface Areas Generated by Functions of Y"} :step/id #uuid "f3f10e55-00c8-543e-a184-f85768a08ce8" :step/math-academy-id 34377 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130113}}] :activity/title "Surface Areas of Revolution: Rotation About the X-Axis" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129339}]}
